From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:45:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: btmtksdio: let the MT7663 Wi-Fi start first

On the Echo Dot (5th gen) the MT7663's Bluetooth and Wi-Fi functions probe
together. Bluetooth's first WMT command came 0.6 ms after Wi-Fi began loading
the ROM patch and N9 firmware; on two cold boots in three both then timed
out ("Execution of wmt command timed out", "Failed to start N9 firmware"),
and neither came up. With Bluetooth's driver kept out of the boot, Wi-Fi
started every time, its N9 running about 0.3 s after probe (C2N6L4,
2026-11-26). Wait 3 s before Bluetooth's MT7663 setup talks to the chip;
the MT7668, which shares the setup, doesn't wait.
It runs from hci_power_on's work, so the wait delays nothing else.

---
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btmtksdio.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btmtksdio.c
@@ -848,6 +848,17 @@
 	int err, status;
 	u8 param = 0x1;
 
+	/*
+	 * The MT7663's Wi-Fi function loads the ROM patch and its N9 firmware
+	 * over the same SDIO card in parallel with this. A WMT command that
+	 * lands while it does times out, and the Wi-Fi start fails with it
+	 * (two cold boots in three on the Echo Dot (5th gen), C2N6L4,
+	 * 2026-11-26). Its N9 runs about 0.3 s after probe: let it finish.
+	 * The MT7668 shares this setup and isn't known to race: it doesn't wait.
+	 */
+	if (bdev->data->chipid == 0x7663)
+		msleep(3000);
+
 	/* Query whether the firmware is already download */
 	wmt_params.op = BTMTK_WMT_SEMAPHORE;
 	wmt_params.flag = 1;
